issue_context.body:
Currently, informers expose a `HasSynced` method.
This asserts that one full List has been synced to the informers *store*.

However, this does not give any guarantees about *handlers* being called.

It is fairly common to need to know that the handlers have been called for the initial state.
However, there is currently no way to access this.

Slack context: https://kubernetes.slack.com/archives/C0EG7JC6T/p1591115369066700

## Sync state

A common question is "What happens if an object is created during the initial sync? Why would we care about the initial state specifically?"

The issue is one of eventually consistency. If I apply, say, a NetworkPolicy, it may not be actuated for some time - its perfectly reasonable for a controller to not process it for O(seconds). However, if I apply a NetworkPolicy 1 year ago, then restart my controller, it *must* be applied; a small blip in policy enforcement is a CVE. A correct controller implementation needs to ensure the initial state is fully synced before operating.

## Current solutions


* The naive option is to just use `informer.HasSynced`. This works if controllers are operating under Listers (or similar) only, rather than event handlers, as the `HasSynced` applies to the underlying store. This is *close enough* that many controllers probably do this while relying on event handlers, improperly, and don't notice that in rare cases the event handlers lag behind.

  Example (broken) implementation:

  <details>

  ```go
  func TestInformerRace(t *testing.T) {
    kc := fake.NewSimpleClientset(pods...)
    informers := informers.NewSharedInformerFactory(kc, 0)
    handled := &atomic.Int32{}
    podInformer := informers.Core().V1().Pods().Informer()
    podInformer.AddEventHandler(cache.ResourceEventHandlerFuncs{
      AddFunc: func(obj any) {
        handled.Add(1)
      },
    })

    stop := make(chan struct{})
    informers.Start(stop)
    WaitForInformerFactorySync(stop, informers)
    WaitForCacheSync(stop, podInformer.HasSynced)

    if h := handled.Load(); h != int32(len(pods)) {
      t.Fatalf("got %+v events, expected %v", h, len(pods))
    }
  }
  ```

  </details>


* After syncing, list all objects. Record all objects we handle, and make sure everything in the initial List call was handled.

	Example (broken) implementation:

  <details>

  ```go
  func TestInformerRaceList(t *testing.T) {
    kc := fake.NewSimpleClientset(pods...)
    informers := informers.NewSharedInformerFactory(kc, 0)
    handled := &atomic.Int32{}
    mu := sync.RWMutex{}
    seenEvents := sets.NewString()
    podInformer := informers.Core().V1().Pods().Informer()
    podInformer.AddEventHandler(cache.ResourceEventHandlerFuncs{
      AddFunc: func(obj any) {
        if k, err := cache.MetaNamespaceKeyFunc(obj); err == nil {
          mu.Lock()
          seenEvents.Insert(k)
          mu.Unlock()
        }
        handled.Add(1)
      },
    })

    stop := make(chan struct{})
    informers.Start(stop)
    WaitForInformerFactorySync(stop, informers)
    WaitForCacheSync(stop, podInformer.HasSynced)

    wantEvents := sets.NewString()
    for _, v := range podInformer.GetStore().List() {
      if k, err := cache.MetaNamespaceKeyFunc(v); err == nil {
        wantEvents.Insert(k)
      }
    }
    for {
      mu.RLock()
      wantEvents = wantEvents.Difference(seenEvents)
      mu.RUnlock()
      if len(wantEvents) == 0 {
        break
      }
      time.Sleep(time.Millisecond)
    }

    if h := handled.Load(); h != int32(len(pods)) {
      t.Fatalf("got %+v events, expected %v", h, len(pods))
    }
  }
  ```

  </details>

  Problems:
	* This is complex and somewhat expensive to maintain
	* It suffers its own race; the List() call may not be the initial set. For example, if a resource was Deleted before the List() call, we may miss the event for that

* After syncing, call List(). For each item, call the handler with that event

  Real world example: https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/e6df935fd30c56a8ece0da6152c60fc725d942b7/pkg/controlplane/controller/crdregistration/crdregistration_controller.go#L120

  Example implementation (not broken, but only works in some cases):

  <details>

  ```go
  func TestInformerRaceListRehandler(t *testing.T) {
    kc := fake.NewSimpleClientset(pods...)
    informers := informers.NewSharedInformerFactory(kc, 0)
    handled := &atomic.Int32{}
    podInformer := informers.Core().V1().Pods().Informer()
    handler := cache.ResourceEventHandlerFuncs{
      AddFunc: func(obj any) {
        handled.Add(1)
      },
    }
    podInformer.AddEventHandler(handler)

    stop := make(chan struct{})
    informers.Start(stop)
    WaitForInformerFactorySync(stop, informers)
    WaitForCacheSync(stop, podInformer.HasSynced)

    for _, v := range podInformer.GetStore().List() {
      handler.OnAdd(v)
    }

    if h := handled.Load(); h < int32(len(pods)) {
      t.Fatalf("got %+v events, expected %v", h, len(pods))
    }
  }
  ```

  </details>

  Problems:
	* Performs poorly; each item is handled multiple times, which may be expensive
	* Only really works for some handler types. The above example is essentially broken; we may be calling an Add event, but a real Delete event came in between; we end up with the wrong state. It *does* work with the fairly common pattern of enqueuing a name on a queue and then reconciling the current state, but this is not something that is done everywhere.

* Bespoke logic. There are many other ways to try to solve this problem. One such example is in the in-tree mutatingWebhookConfigurationManager (which is incorrect, but would be trivially solved by the addition of changes in this PR: https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/issues/113761)

## Proposed solution

Ultimately, the ask is to expose a mechanism to know that a *handler* has been called for the initial state.

From an API perspective, this could be exposed at the Informer level, or the handler level. While the Informer level is more intuitive from an API perspective, it is a bit odd to ask "Have all handlers been synced"; most callers only care about *their* handler. However, there is not currently a way to access a specific handler in the API today, so its tricky to expose a `handler.HasSynced()` function, or similar.

### Determining when a handler is synced

Ultimately, the point in the code where we disconnect from the underlying store knowing it has synced but the handler not being synced is in processorListener. In `processorListener.add`, we send on `addCh`, which then basically acts as an unbounded ring buffer between `nextCh`. The important part is it is asynchronous, which is why all of our problems exists.

**Option 1: Make processorListener synchronous**

Presumably, this is async because we expect handlers may be slow. However, I think there is some general guidance that handlers should be fast (this is why most handlers are just `Enqueue()`) (although I couldn't find this written down anywhere...). Because of that, it may be feasible to just make `processorListener` synchronous.

From a code standpoint, this is pretty simple:

```diff
@@ -770,7 +802,19 @@ func newProcessListener(handler ResourceEventHandler, requestedResyncPeriod, res
 }

 func (p *processorListener) add(notification interface{}) {-
-       p.addCh <- notification
+       switch notification := notification.(type) {
+       case updateNotification:
+               p.handler.OnUpdate(notification.oldObj, notification.newObj)
+       case addNotification:
+               p.handler.OnAdd(notification.newObj)
+       case deleteNotification:
+               p.handler.OnDelete(notification.oldObj)
+       default:
+               utilruntime.HandleError(fmt.Errorf("unrecognized notification: %T", notification))
+       }
 }
```

However, in practice this may have fairly substantial impacts and unknown-unknowns. Its plausible this could be an explicit opt-in, though, to mitigate backwards compatibility.

By making this synchronous, we put processorListener, and thus the handler, under the same semantics as the underlying Store syncing (as handled by `processDeltas`), which means `informer.HasSynced()` will now ensure that both the underlying Store is synced to the initial state *and* the handler has been called for the initial state.

Informer users would then just the current "naive solution" of calling `informer.HasSynced()`.

**Option 2: Expose a processorListener.HasSynced method**

This option would involve exposing a way for a processorListener to instead explicitly report whether it has synced full or not (but still processes events async).

The current interface between DeltaFIFO (the thing that knows when we are synced) and processorListener (the thing we want to know) interfaces through `sharedIndexInformer.OnAdd` which calls `s.processor.distribute`. In order to propagate the state between these, we need to expose a bit more information through these interfaces.

