Essentially, DeltaFIFO knows when it is processing the last item to be fully synced. When it encounters this, it propogates some additional info through to the processorListener. When the processorListener processes the `syncNotification`, it marks itself as synced.

### Exposing if a handler was synced

Fundamentally, most clients will want to be able to answer "Has *my* handler synced"; they don't care about "Have *all handlers registered* for the informer synced". Depending on our implementation, this may guide our API decision.

The easy approach is to just expose this at the informer level (either modify `HasSynced` semantics, or more likely add a new `HaveAllHandlersSynced`). For "Option 1" above, this is really our only option because `informer.HasSynced` implies `informer.HaveAllHandlersSynced`. For "Option 2" above, we could chose to expose it on a per handler basis or aggregate all handlers and expose it at a listener level.

The issue with exposing it at a handler level is there is not an existing API interface to hang this new function on. `AddEventHandler` just takes a ResourceEventHandler and returns nothing. We could add alternative function calls that either take in or return some callback for HasSynced, though; there is some prior are for alternative functions here with `AddEventHandlerWithResyncPeriod`.

If we don't want to change the API shape at all, we could also keep the existing API, but add a new `ResourceEventHandlerWithSyncedCallback` which would have a new function added, then see if the passed in handler implements that and register the callback that way. However, I think doing this is generally frowned upon.


